- 2 renamed pages;
- 3 broken in-book links, which no test reads;
- rollup headings using ruled titles for pages that keep their old ones;
- index.json going stale (see index-json-dangles).
The spec's '24' is the critic's whole-chapter count (W2: 7/4/5/8) applied to a per-module run; the measured figure is 6. The slug map is not swap-only either: the committed map already held the REVERSE entries for 11-4 (recorded 2026-09-20) and 19-1 (2026-09-21), and the run flips them.
Control: the slug-map-corpus predicate still passes on the after-tree (each 'to' exists and its moduleId matches), while the index predicate fails, so the instruments tell the two apart.
Mitigations: the sync is held, and the register puts ② 'BEFORE THE SYNC', so readers see this only if that order is broken. The run order is still wrong in its stated expectation and cannot land cleanly. No clean retire-only path exists before ②: whole-chapter inject is red today.

D10 finds candidates 'by walking the tree' and requires that 'no other file under 03-translated/ or 05-publication/ … contains its name'. That admits a disk-walking reference scan. Under that reading:
- all 15 targets are kept;
- 'its first run also clears the 9 June leftovers' is false;
- step 5 cannot pass on this box;
- a D11 test that walks the disk fails locally and passes in CI.
The step-3 re-inject itself mints a fresh backup that still holds the old reference, so the disk-walk reading fails for the retirees even on a clean clone.
Downgraded from major: the result is fail-closed (files are kept and listed with their reasons), and D11's CI test on a clean clone would flag the surviving published copies of retirees. A one-line clarification fixes it: tracked files only, or exclude render's isPublicationArtifact set.
